<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phra That Si Song Rak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1560">1560</a>–<a href="/years/1563">1563</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phra That Si <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Rak is a stupa at Dan Sai in Loei Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, built between 1560 and 1563 to commemorate the alliance between King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setthathirath">Setthathirath</a> of Lan Xang and King Maha Chakkraphat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. Its name means &quot;stupa of the love of two&quot;, and it marked the border between the two kingdoms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phra-that-si-song-rak.jpg" alt="Phra That Si Song Rak" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Oatz assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phra_That_Si_Song_Rak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
